The edges of $\Omega^+(v)$ are precisely those of the form $[n,i] - [n-1,j]$ for all $i \in \interval{n}, j \in \interval{n-1}$, along with all $[k,i] - 
[k-1,j]$ for $1 \leq i \leq j \leq k \leq n - 1$. Recall from \S<<ref:subsec-socle>> that $\overline \P (\Omega^+(v))$ is the set of those $z 
\in \DD(\mathbf 0)$ such that $\Omega^+(v) \subset \Omega^+(z)$. We will prove that the support of $M(-\rho)$ is equal to the set of integral points $\overline \P (\Omega^+(v))$.

\begin{Theorem} Let $z \in \DD(\mathbf 0)$. Then $z$ lies in the Gelfand-Tsetlin support of $M(-\rho)$ if and only if $z_{k-1,j} \leq z_{k,i} \leq 0$ for all $k \leq n-1$ and $i \leq j$. Equivalently, the support of $M(-\rho)$ coincides with $\overline \P(\Omega^+(v))$. \end{Theorem}
